{"title":"Ishika's Garden Secret","gradeSubjectTag":"Class 5 · Science · Photosynthesis","readMinutes":2,"scenes":[{"label":"hook","text":"Ishika was helping her grandmother water the plants in their terrace garden on a sunny morning. She noticed that all the leaves were bright green, and wondered why they were never red or yellow like the flowers."},{"label":"problem","text":"'Grandmother, why are leaves always green?' Ishika asked. Her grandmother smiled and said, 'That's because leaves have a special job to do with the sunlight. Let me show you.'"},{"label":"mathMoment","text":"Grandmother explained: 'Inside each leaf is something called chlorophyll, which is green in colour. This chlorophyll captures light energy from the sun. When sunlight falls on the leaf, the chlorophyll absorbs all the colours of the rainbow, but it reflects the green light back—that's why we see green. The plant uses the other colours of light energy to make food from air and water. That's why leaves need to be green to do their job properly.'"},{"label":"wrapUp","text":"Ishika looked at the leaves with new eyes and smiled. 'So the green colour is the leaf's special uniform for catching sunlight and making food!' She watered each plant carefully, knowing now that every green leaf was hard at work in the sunshine, feeding the plant and keeping it healthy."}],"visual":null,"equations":[],"tryTogether":{"question":"Why do you think a leaf that turns brown or yellow cannot make food for the plant as well as a green leaf can?","answer":"Because brown and yellow leaves have little or no chlorophyll, so they cannot capture and use light energy properly."},"parentPrompt":"Ask Ishika to look at a real plant leaf in sunlight and describe what she sees the green colour doing."}
